<article class="env-definition" id="048Q">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/048Q">Definition <span data-tag="048Q">83.5.4</span>.</a> Let $S$ be a scheme, and let $B$ be an algebraic space over $S$. Let $j : R \to U \times _ B U$ be a pre-relation over $B$. Let $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(k) \to B$ be a geometric point of $B$. </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
We say $\overline{u}, \overline{u}' \in U(k)$ are <em>weakly $R$-equivalent</em> if they are in the same equivalence class for the equivalence relation generated by the relation $j(R(k)) \subset U(k) \times U(k)$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
We say $\overline{u}, \overline{u}' \in U(k)$ are <em>$R$-equivalent</em> if for some overfield $k \subset \Omega $ the images in $U(\Omega )$ are weakly $R$-equivalent. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
The <em>weak orbit</em>, or more precisely the <em>weak $R$-orbit</em> of $\overline{u} \in U(k)$ is set of all elements of $U(k)$ which are weakly $R$-equivalent to $\overline{u}$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
The <em>orbit</em>, or more precisely the <em>$R$-orbit</em> of $\overline{u} \in U(k)$ is set of all elements of $U(k)$ which are $R$-equivalent to $\overline{u}$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article>